KG42.register("rules-67a760efb73d6e60f6c717da",{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R005","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R005","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Price-signal coordination","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"Local market price information informs decentralized trading decisions, and EMTS may respond to variable prices through demand-side management. Scope: General microgrid EMTS design and the BMG virtual market's provision of local price information; demand-side management is a general design possibility. For the illustrative simple EMTS, the decentralized decision to buy energy on the microgrid market is triggered whenever the market price falls below its maximum price limit. Scope: Illustrative simple EMTS strategy in the general microgrid market design. Condition: The microgrid market price falls below the EMTS's maximum price limit. Price signals should indicate energy scarcity or surplus. Scope: General microgrid market price-signal design.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.price_signal under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.price_signal","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"pricing.local","type":"conditional_on","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E002","source":"pricing.local","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06","v141-b-g02-e22"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"General microgrid EMTS design and the BMG virtual market's provision of local price information; demand-side management is a general design possibility. | General microgrid market price-signal design. | Illustrative simple EMTS strategy in the general microgrid market design.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e22"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R005-x001","symbol":"buy_i","meaning":"Decentralized purchase decision of EMTS/market participant i for the illustrative simple EMTS strategy; 1 denotes buying energy on the microgrid market and 0 denotes not buying. The source describes the action but provides no explicit mathematical symbol.","kind":"operational","domain":"binary {0,1}; formalized purchase action, source gives no explicit notation","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta001","symbol":"p_M","meaning":"Microgrid local market energy price observed by the EMTS when applying the simple buying rule; descriptive symbol because the supplied text gives no explicit notation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta002","symbol":"p_i_max","meaning":"Maximum price limit of EMTS/market participant i for buying energy on the microgrid market; descriptive symbol because the supplied text gives no explicit notation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local market price information informs decentralized trading decisions, and EMTS may respond to variable prices through demand-side management. Scope: General microgrid EMTS design and the BMG virtual market's provision of local price information; demand-side management is a general design possibility.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06","v141-b-g02-e22"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"For the illustrative simple EMTS, the decentralized decision to buy energy on the microgrid market is triggered whenever the market price falls below its maximum price limit. Scope: Illustrative simple EMTS strategy in the general microgrid market design. Condition: The microgrid market price falls below the EMTS's maximum price limit.","expression":"p_M \u003c p_i_max => buy_i = 1","formalization_origin":"equivalent_translation","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R005-x001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R005-theta001","v141-c-R005-theta002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e06"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Price signals should indicate energy scarcity or surplus. Scope: General microgrid market price-signal design.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.6 Energy management trading system (EMTS) (C6)","anchor_text":"The EMTS’s main objective is to automatically secure the energy supply for a market participant whil","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The EMTS’s main objective is to automatically secure the energy supply for a market participant while implementing a specific bidding strategy. The EMTS needs access to the (real time) demand and supply data of its market participant. Based on this data the EMTS forecasts consumption and generation, and develops its bidding strategy accordingly. Furthermore, it trades the predicted amounts on the market platform and may react to variable prices via demand side management [53]. Self-interested rational market participants maximize their revenue and minimize their energy costs. A simple EMTS would always buy energy at the microgrid market when the price falls below its maximum price limit. However, individual agents’ intelligent bidding strategies shall employ varying prices at different times and are one of the core components of active local markets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.5 Pricing mechanism (C5)","anchor_text":"The pricing mechanism is implemented via the market mecha nism (C4) and aims at efﬁciently allocatin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Price signals should be used to indicate energy scarcity or surplus [31]. Accordingly, a surplus of energy should lower the microgrid’s energy price while a lack of local energy increases the market price. Economically speaking, local markets are beneficial to their participants as long as the average energy price is lower than the external grid price. However, if socio-economic reasons (e.g. supporting local renewable energy) are considered, the local energy price may even surpass the grid price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e22","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the TransActive Grid private blockchain protocol (C3). Thus, the characteristics of data consistency, security, and verification are covered by the TransActive Grid platform and are inherited traits from the blockchain protocol. Based on measurements of demand and supply as well as market information, the platform tracks and displays local (near) real time energy prices on an intuitive interface. Thus, market participants may gather generation, consumption and market information, and trade energy needs accordingly. Trading is mostly done automatically by the EMTS (C6) and just requires several preferences of its market participants, e.g. the preferred energy sources and price limits."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e22","quote":"Based on measurements of demand and supply as well as market information, the platform tracks and displays local (near) real time energy prices on an intuitive interface. Thus, market participants may gather generation, consumption and market information, and trade energy needs accordingly."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e06","quote":"Furthermore, it trades the predicted amounts on the market platform and may react to variable prices via demand side management [53]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e06","quote":"A simple EMTS would always buy energy at the microgrid market when the price falls below its maximum price limit."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","quote":"Price signals should be used to indicate energy scarcity or surplus [31]."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"a994d67b3e5b5516b80cb1f6c842a6cddcedbc0320993a765d9b917f2f0f9b38","applicability":"formalized","reason":"C002 supplies a clear source-supported trigger condition for the illustrative simple EMTS: buy energy on the microgrid market when the market price falls below the EMTS participant's maximum price limit. It is formalized as a one-way implication, preserving 'falls below' as strict '\u003c' and 'always buy when' as a sufficient condition rather than a biconditional. C001 and C003 remain not_formalized because the supplied text is procedural/qualitative and gives no explicit variables, domains, functional form, or quantitative relation. B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-x001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"The source sentence makes the EMTS purchase action the decentralized response to a price comparison. In the frozen graph this response is represented by coordination.price_signal, and E001 captures its conditional dependence on pricing.local under C002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-theta001","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"The microgrid market price is the local price condition in C002. It is bound to pricing.local, with E001 representing the frozen conditional relation between the coordination response and the local price."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-theta002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"The maximum price limit is the participant-specific threshold used by the EMTS purchase decision. Because the graph has no separate preference node, it is bound to the coordinating decision node coordination.price_signal and to the C002 conditional edge E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"C002 is formalized as the one-way trigger p_M \u003c p_i_max => buy_i = 1. The two members are the coordination response and local price nodes, and E001 is the frozen edge whose constraint_ids identify C002."}],"revision_requests":["B evidence remains unreviewed. The supplied text for C002 gives no explicit source symbols, units, or domains for the microgrid market price and the EMTS maximum price limit, so descriptive formal symbols p_M, p_i_max, and buy_i are used. A B source re-extraction/audit should confirm the exact notation before these symbols are treated as canonical."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
